\title{Tips on solving Kattis problems}
\date{2026-08-27T16:06:28Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Throughout the semester, I will occasionally assign programming
challenges from [Open Kattis](https://open.kattis.com/).  These
problems will help you translate theory into practice by writing
working implementations of algorithms or concepts we have been
discussing in class.}

\p{I am very aware that solutions to Kattis problems can easily be
found online, and LLMs can solve many of them.  I trust you not to
look at any such solutions.  You only get the benefit of these
problems by solving them on your own (not to mention that doing
otherwise would be an academic integrity violation).}

\subtree{\title{Solving} \p{You can implement your solution in any
  programming language you wish.  Kattis always expects you to submit
  a program that \em{reads from standard input} and \em{writes to
  standard output}.  For example, in Python, that means using
  \tt{input()} to read the input and \tt{print()} to produce output.
  In Java, you can use \tt{Scanner} and \tt{System.out.println}, and so
  on.}

  \p{If you'd like additional hints on how to parse the input, how to
  access standard data structures in your chosen programming language,
  \em{etc.}, just ask!}
}

\subtree{\title{Submitting}
  \p{To submit your solution to a Kattis problem, you must submit \em{both}:
  \ul{
    \li{A screenshot showing that Kattis accepted your submission}
    \li{Your code. Note, do \em{not} submit a screenshot of your code, and do not submit a project folder containing a zillion auto-generated files!  Just submit a single \tt{.py} or \tt{.java} file (for example—you can also use other languages besides Python and Java).}
  }
  }
}
